    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\nSame construction extends to $\\mathbb{R}^n$: cover by *boxes* $\\prod_{i=1}^n (a_i, b_i)$, use the obvious volume $\\prod (b_i - a_i)$ as the basis. Carathéodory carries through, giving Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nThe Lebesgue σ-algebra is invariant under:\n- Translation: $\\lambda(E + t) = \\lambda(E)$.\n- Reflection: $\\lambda(-E) = \\lambda(E)$.\n- Linear maps: $\\lambda(T E) = |\\det T| \\lambda(E)$ — the Jacobian determinant from Stage 2/05.\n\n## What we got\n\n- An σ-algebra $\\mathcal{L}$ much larger than Borel, containing every Borel set and every subset of a Borel measure-zero set.\n- A measure $\\lambda$ extending the natural notion of length / area / volume.\n- A non-measurable example (Vitali) showing why we can't have everything.\n- A null + uncountable example (Cantor) showing measure isn't cardinality.\n\nThe next notebook builds the **integral** on top of this — a tour de force of constructing a robust, well-behaved integration theory by approximating from below with simple functions.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nStein, E.M. and Shakarchi, R. (2005) *Real Analysis: Measure Theory, Integration, and Hilbert Spaces*. Princeton Lectures in Analysis III. Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press — ch. 1, *Measure Theory* (outer measure, Carathéodory, Vitali, Cantor).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
